A zero-timeout mutex take polls, as in FreeRTOS

Applied by scripts/patch_simulator.py after 0001. Task R4a (#21).

src/freertos/semphr.h: xSemaphoreTake() on a mutex
  The shim ignored the timeout and always blocked. Firmware code that polls
  the render mutex with a zero timeout, RenderLock(RenderLock::Mode::Try)
  (src/activities/ActivityManager.cpp; used in src/main.cpp's loop() and by
  the reader), then waited for the whole render instead of carrying on. With
  RSVP's display timing model a render holds the mutex for a modelled 540 ms
  word update, so the simulated main loop, which reads keys and touch,
  stopped for that long on every word; on the device it does not. A zero
  timeout now uses try_lock() and fails at once when another task holds the
  mutex. Other timeouts keep the old blocking behaviour.

diff --git a/src/freertos/semphr.h b/src/freertos/semphr.h
index e4e88ab..744ce6a 100644
--- a/src/freertos/semphr.h
+++ b/src/freertos/semphr.h
@@ -97,7 +97,13 @@ inline bool xSemaphoreTake(SemaphoreHandle_t sem, uint32_t ticksToWait) {
     return true;
   }
   auto *mtx = sim_semaphore_detail::asMutex(sem);
-  mtx->mtx.lock();
+  if (ticksToWait == 0) {
+    // A zero timeout polls, as in FreeRTOS: fail at once if another task holds it.
+    if (!mtx->mtx.try_lock())
+      return false;
+  } else {
+    mtx->mtx.lock();
+  }
   mtx->holder = xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle();
   mtx->holdCount++;
   return true;
